# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROD environment — Arbitrum One (chainId 42161), real Chainlink Data Streams
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env.prod
#   npm run simulate:prod     # dry run, no broadcast — always do this first
#   npm run deploy:prod       # prompts for confirmation before broadcasting
#
# DEPLOY_LABEL is not set here — the npm targets pass it inline (dev/prod) to
# name the deployments/<label>-<block>.json record. Invoking `forge script`
# by hand without it writes `unlabeled-<block>.json`.
#
# This deploys against REAL USDT with REAL user funds. The live prod Settlement
# held 557.24 USDT across 14,267 markets as of 2026-08-10 and was still trading
# while these values were read. Treat every step as a live-money operation.
#
# Every non-secret value below was read off the live prod contracts and is the
# exact config the current deployment runs with. Deploying from this file
# reproduces prod like-for-like: Settlement, Resolver and AutoCycler are all
# logic-identical to the live deployment (zero residual diffs with immutables +
# metadata masked), re-verified after the 9a7b3d8 formatting pass.
#
# TWAP 60s CUTOVER COMPLETE (2026-08-14). STREAMS_FEED_ID_BTC_USD / _ETH_USD
# point at Chainlink's TWAP: 60s streams, and the live prod resolver
# (0x8fae4F24…2076c, deployments/prod-25755446.json) holds exactly these ids.
# This file mirrors live again with no exceptions. Verified in production:
# StrikeCaptured and MarketResolved on both pairs, zero ResolveFailed.
#
# The entitlement for these streams was granted on 2026-08-14; both ids had
# returned HTTP 401 "feeds not authorized" on 08-13 and again earlier the same
# day. Chainlink stated the grant had landed a day before it actually did — see
# the STREAMS_FEED_ID block for why that claim is worth re-testing, not
# trusting.
#
# The cutover ran with --skip-drain and orphaned 6 open markets, one of which
# took 62.50 USDT of real positions 19 seconds after the drain gate read it as
# empty. All six were recovered via registerMarket on the new resolver. See
# deployments/README.md for the timeline before using that flag again.
#
# The two "blockers" at the bottom of this file were both ANSWERED on
# 2026-08-13 and are kept only as a record: the Chainlink allow-list is not
# per-address (a fresh resolver resolved immediately), and the zero LINK
# balance is correct because the FeeManager quotes a zero fee for this account.
#
# BEFORE YOU DEPLOY, run: npm run preflight:prod  — and use `npm run
# upgrade:prod`, NOT `deploy:prod`. On 2026-08-13 deploy:prod was run directly,
# skipping the drain, and orphaned 6 live markets holding 221.63 USDT.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Secrets — GENERATE FRESH, never reuse the previous developer's keys ──────
# 0x-prefixed hex. Deploy.s.sol reads this with vm.envUint, so the 0x is
# required. This key becomes the initial owner of all three contracts; hand
# ownership to the Safe afterwards (see OWNER_ADDRESS below).
# Fund it with ETH on Arbitrum One before deploying.
DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x

ARBITRUM_RPC_URL=
ARBISCAN_API_KEY=

# ── Roles — GENERATE FRESH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Relayer: hot key held by the backend; submits enterPosition / mint / redeemFor.
# This is the key most exposed to the previous developer — rotate it first and
# make sure the backend is redeployed with the new one before cutover.
RELAYER_ADDRESS=0x
# Treasury: funds rebate claims, and is the fallback sink for a round's fee when
# the buyback cannot complete (see the buyback block below). It NO LONGER receives
# platformFee on each fill — that accrues per round and is burned at resolution.
# Still must be non-zero: with no treasury a failed buyback defers the fee back to
# its own round instead, which is safe but needs a manual buybackAndBurn to clear.
# Confirm who controls the outgoing treasury EOA before deciding whether this
# needs to change; it may belong to the client rather than the old developer.
TREASURY_ADDRESS=0x

# ── Fee buyback-and-burn (RAIN) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# platformFee no longer leaves on each fill. It accrues inside the Settlement,
# booked to the round that earned it, and when that round resolves the contract
# swaps it for RAIN on Uniswap V3 and burns it — the same swap-then-burn the RAIN
# protocol runs in LibUtils.swapAndBurn, moved from "first claim" to "round end".
#
# OPTIONAL. Leave RAIN_TOKEN_ADDRESS empty and Deploy.s.sol skips the route: fees
# still accrue and each resolve forwards them to TREASURY_ADDRESS instead, so
# nothing is stranded and nothing is burned. Set it later with setBuybackRoute.
#
# The deploy composes the path as USDT -> WETH -> RAIN. setBuybackRoute validates
# that it starts at USDT and ends at the burn token, so a wrong pair fails the
# deploy rather than surfacing as silent BuybackFallbackToTreasury events.
RAIN_TOKEN_ADDRESS=0x25118290e6A5f4139381D072181157035864099d
# Defaults below are the Arbitrum One canonical addresses; override only to test.
# NOTE: the canonical SwapRouter is required, NOT SwapRouter02 — the ISwapRouter
# the Settlement calls carries a `deadline` field that SwapRouter02 dropped.
UNISWAP_V3_ROUTER=0xE592427A0AEce92De3Edee1F18E0157C05861564
UNISWAP_V3_QUOTER=0xb27308f9F90D607463bb33eA1BeBb41C27CE5AB6
WETH_ADDRESS=0x82aF49447D8a07e3bd95BD0d56f35241523fBab1
# Pool fee tiers for the two hops. RAIN/WETH is the 0.01% tier (100), matching the
# RAIN protocol's own RAIN_WETH_FEE constant.
BUYBACK_USDT_WETH_FEE=500
BUYBACK_WETH_RAIN_FEE=100
# THE burn caller. There is no automatic on-chain trigger: this keeper calls
# buybackAndBurn(marketIds) on finished rounds, on whatever cadence it likes
# (hourly batches beat one swap per round). Gated on purpose — the caller picks
# when the swap runs and the slippage floor is quoted in-transaction, so a
# permissionless caller could sandwich it (measured: 94% of a 5,000 USDT burn
# destroyed, attacker up ~2,528 USDT). It is an ALLOW-LIST, so more keepers can
# be added later with setBuybackExecutor(addr, true). The owner can always call it; defaults to RELAYER_ADDRESS when unset.
BUYBACK_EXECUTOR_ADDRESS=

# ── Collateral + feeds (live prod values, carry over unchanged) ──────────────
# Real USDT on Arbitrum One.
USDT_ADDRESS=0xFd086bC7CD5C481DCC9C85ebE478A1C0b69FCbb9

# REAL Chainlink Data Streams VerifierProxy on Arbitrum One. Read off the live
# prod resolver — this is the genuine one, not a mock.
CHAINLINK_VERIFIER_PROXY_ADDRESS=0x478Aa2aC9F6D65F84e09D9185d126c3a17c2a93C

CHAINLINK_LINK_TOKEN_ADDRESS=0xf97f4df75117a78c1A5a0DBb814Af92458539FB4
CHAINLINK_BTC_USD_FEED=0x6ce185860a4963106506C203335A2910413708e9
CHAINLINK_ETH_USD_FEED=0x639Fe6ab55C921f74e7fac1ee960C0B6293ba612
CHAINLINK_SEQUENCER_FEED=0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D

# ── Migration mode — SET for a Resolver/AutoCycler upgrade ──────────────────
# Skips the Settlement deploy and redeploys only Resolver + AutoCycler, then
# rewires the existing Settlement via setResolver/setAutocycler. Every market,
# user position and USDT balance stays exactly where it is.
#
# SET IT   when changing resolver or cycler logic. This is the normal upgrade.
#          Leaving it unset there deploys an EMPTY Settlement while all real
#          user collateral stays stranded on the old one.
#
# UNSET IT only when rotating away from an owner key you no longer trust.
#          Preserving the old Settlement leaves that owner able to drain
#          collateral via proposeEmergencyWithdraw, so that case wants a fresh
#          Settlement plus a deliberate user migration. (An earlier version of
#          this comment said "LEAVE UNSET / fresh deploy only" without the
#          distinction, which reads as an unconditional ban and is wrong for
#          every upgrade.)
#
# Before deploying, run: npm run preflight:prod
# It refuses to proceed while any market is still open — repointing the
# resolver mid-market orphans it and locks whatever collateral it holds.
#
# The address below is the live Arbitrum One Settlement as of 2026-08-14,
# matching deployments/prod-25755446.json and confirmed against chain
# (settlement.resolver / .autocycler / resolver.trustedSettlement all agree).
# Settlement itself is long-lived; the record cited here tracks the CURRENT
# resolver/autocycler pair, so it moves with every migration-mode redeploy.
# Its owner is 0xCEcEF153149b7D8eC55e6420B0FF342127df3c32 — your
# DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY must resolve to that address or the deploy reverts
# mid-broadcast, after the new contracts are already paid for.
EXISTING_SETTLEMENT_ADDRESS=0x4B662f68BD6C40e192e28C1045E829A4B10C754e

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEPLOY-TIME — read by Deploy.s.sol inside the broadcast
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REQUIRED. Chainlink TWAP: 60s stream ids — LIVE ON CHAIN since 2026-08-14.
# Catalog entries `BTC / USD - TWAP: 60s` and `ETH / USD - TWAP: 60s`
# (product names BTC|ETH/USD-Streams-TWAP-60s-DS-Premium-Global-002).
# All dates in this block are UTC, matching the chain timestamps.
#
# The deployed resolver (0x8fae4F24…2076c) holds exactly these, and both pairs
# are capturing strikes and resolving in production with zero ResolveFailed.
#
# Entitlement history, because it is the trap here. Both ids returned HTTP 401
# "feeds not authorized" on 2026-08-13 and AGAIN on 2026-08-14 after Chainlink
# stated the account had been granted them — in the same runs, with the same
# credentials, the 30s control returned HTTP 200, so it was never creds, host
# or a malformed id. The grant actually landed later on 2026-08-14. Treat any
# entitlement claim as unverified until a fetch proves it.
#
# RE-CHECK BEFORE ANY DEPLOY, don't trust this line. Entitlement is account
# state on Chainlink's side, it has already flipped once, and nothing local
# detects a revocation: the ids are 32 bytes and schema v2, so
# UpgradePreflight._checkNewStreamIds passes them and configureStreamsFeed
# accepts them REGARDLESS — an unentitled id yields a resolver that opens
# markets, takes real collateral, and only fails at the first captureStrike.
# Gate the broadcast on a fresh 200 for BOTH ids:
#   node script/fetch_streams_report.mjs <feedId> latest --pretty
# Expect 200, "schema": "v2", a 224-byte report with no bid/ask, sane price.
# (A 200 proves entitlement and that the stream is live; the report body has no
# window field, so it does NOT prove these are the 60s entries rather than
# another TWAP. Match the ids against the catalog names above for that.)
#
# ROLLBACK — the TWAP: 30s ids these replace, live on chain 2026-08-13 to
# 2026-08-14 and confirmed working in production over that window. Restore
# these two lines verbatim to go back:
#   STREAMS_FEED_ID_BTC_USD=0x0002e6b03af5a87b65f4c5c0c8eaf6027e4156f467cd16bdcf8a5b4347f9c087
#   STREAMS_FEED_ID_ETH_USD=0x00020ee5ba8c3b643145974fd94552d591f2a3bb165c196ccf4c30ba600fbffd
# Those in turn superseded the v3 SPOT ids (0x00039d9e… / 0x000362205e…).
#
# The leading two bytes are the REPORT SCHEMA, not a genuineness marker (an
# earlier version of this comment said "the 0x0003… prefix marks them as
# genuine DON stream IDs", which is wrong and has already misled a reader).
# 0x0003 = v3, price + simulated bid/ask. 0x0002 = v2, price only — what the
# TWAP streams publish under, and every bit as genuine. The resolver decodes
# both; configureStreamsFeed rejects anything else. The prefix alone does not
# tell you spot vs TWAP — check the catalog entry, or fetch a report: only the
# v3 spot streams carry bid/ask.
#
# 60s WINDOW ON ALL THREE TIMEFRAMES — deliberate, exactly as the 30s setup it
# replaces was. streamsFeedId is keyed by pairId alone, so 300s / 900s / 3600s
# markets all price off one window. Going to 60s MOVES the Polymarket mismatch
# rather than removing it: PM uses 60s for its 15m and 4h markets, so 900s /
# 3600s become PM-parity and 300s becomes the timeframe that diverges (the
# reverse of the 30s setup, where only 300s matched). Matching PM on all three
# needs per-timeframe stream config, which is a resolver change and is not
# planned. Revisit only if that becomes a product requirement.
#
# IF YOU EVER CHANGE THESE TWO LINES, DRAIN FIRST. resolve() reads
# streamsFeedId at settlement time while the strike was captured earlier under
# whatever was configured then, so flipping with markets open strikes them on
# one source and settles them on another — a wrong-side payout that reverts
# nothing. npm run preflight:prod refuses to proceed while any market is open.
# On 2026-08-13 that gate was bypassed (deploy:prod run directly instead of
# upgrade:prod) and it orphaned 6 markets holding 221.63 USDT; recovery needed
# a manual registerMarket on the new resolver for each.
#
# Deploy.s.sol calls configureStreamsFeed for both pairs during the deploy; an
# unset value aborts before anything is broadcast. Do NOT leave these blank
# intending to apply them by hand — a prod resolver with no Streams config
# opens markets, takes real collateral, and can never settle it.
STREAMS_FEED_ID_BTC_USD=0x0002ee6757e8822c00d273bc340fc24c9cafe123a4ff2ea1dbdb31944bc7d95f
STREAMS_FEED_ID_ETH_USD=0x0002fbf9f58ef89c93385770d289d7c10c01c250641daa811063e303ee095593

# REQUIRED ON PROD. Cycler forwarder, applied by Deploy.s.sol. Prod runs the
# keeper from a SEPARATE EOA, so this MUST be set — left blank it silently
# defaults to RELAYER_ADDRESS, collapsing the keeper and relayer onto one key,
# which is the opposite of the separation prod has today. Generate fresh.
# (The outgoing forwarder is a plain EOA, not a Chainlink Automation forwarder
# contract — prod is on the cron stopgap, not Automation.)
KEEPER_FORWARDER_ADDRESS=0x

# Optional. Cycler pre-listing window, applied by Deploy.s.sol. Defaults to 300,
# matching the live prod deployment. The contract's own default is 0 and the
# constructor does not set it, so this exists to stop a fresh deploy from
# silently regressing and leaving market makers no pre-positioning window.
# Max 300 (PRE_START_WINDOW_MAX); leave blank unless you have a reason.
# PRE_START_WINDOW_SEC=300

# Final owner, transferred by Deploy.s.sol as its last broadcast step. All three
# contracts are Ownable2Step, so this only sets pendingOwner and the target must
# call acceptOwnership() to complete it — a wrong address cannot lock you out.
# Left blank, the deployer key stays owner and the script warns.
# Use a Safe multisig: owner can rewire the resolver and drain collateral, so a
# single hot key is what you are trying to get away from.
# There is deliberately no OWNER_PRIVATE_KEY on prod — nothing here signs the
# acceptance locally. Take the three contract addresses from the deploy output
# (also written to deployments/prod-<l1Block>.json) and call acceptOwnership()
# from the Safe UI.
# (This used to point at `npm run accept-ownership:prod`; that target was
# removed in 6f03a35 and the reference was left behind.)
OWNER_ADDRESS=0x

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POST-DEPLOY — nothing to configure here
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The deploy prints the three contract addresses and writes them to
# deployments/prod-<block>.json. Submit `acceptOwnership()` to each from the
# Safe at OWNER_ADDRESS to complete the Ownable2Step handoff — paste from that
# record rather than keeping a third copy here that can drift.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BLOCKERS — BOTH ANSWERED EMPIRICALLY ON 2026-08-13, kept for the record
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The 2026-08-13 TWAP cutover deployed a brand-new resolver
# (0x9B524376…885a) and it began capturing strikes and resolving markets
# IMMEDIATELY, with no allow-list request and a zero LINK balance:
# 14 StrikeCaptured, 12+ MarketResolved, 0 ResolveFailed within minutes.
# That settles both questions below. Re-verify if Chainlink changes billing.
#
# 1. CHAINLINK ALLOW-LIST — NOT a per-address blocker, as feared.
#    The concern was that a freshly deployed resolver would fail verify() until
#    Chainlink allow-listed the new address. It did not: authorization is tied
#    to the Data Streams ACCOUNT/credentials, not the consumer contract address.
#    No request was opened and resolution worked on the first market. Note this
#    is about the on-chain consumer; per-STREAM entitlement on the API side is
#    real and separate — it is what blocked the TWAP: 60s streams (HTTP 401 on
#    2026-08-13 and again on 2026-08-14, despite Chainlink saying the account
#    had already been granted them; the grant actually landed later that day).
#    Verify any entitlement claim with fetch_streams_report.mjs rather than
#    acting on it — the claim led the actual grant by a day here.
#
# 2. LINK FUNDING — the FeeManager returns a ZERO fee for this account.
#    The open question was whether zero LINK meant zero fees or meant Streams
#    verification was not really on the resolution path. It is definitively on
#    the path (StrikeCaptured/MarketResolved are emitted only after
#    verifierProxy.verify succeeds), and the resolver still holds 0 LINK — so
#    fees are zero for us on Arbitrum One. No top-up is needed. If Chainlink
#    ever starts charging, every captureStrike/resolve reverts at once; budget
#    the 5–10 LINK floor cited in Deploy.s.sol:140 and top up by direct
#    transfer:  LINK.transfer(resolver, amount)